Findings: [minor] QC-01 (D-01/D-03 consistency) settings/CalloutPopup.lua:197 (showError, NS.SkinEscape(ERROR_TOKEN)) and :207-208 (showTokenHelp); settings/ModalWindow.lua header comment ('The page inside and its controls follow the skin as every settings page does (stock while the owner ignores its settings panel)'): The QC-01 extension of D-03 makes the settings pages consistent, but leaves the callout popup's body mixed. The popup's AceGUI body follows the panel Scope: by the ModalWindow comment it is stock under the default Ignore settings panel. Its refused-field line (sem.badAlt, an AceGUI row in that body) and its token tooltip read the live skin with no panel flag. With a non-Ka0s skin and the default Scope, the popup shows stock AceGUI controls with a skin-colored error line. That is the one-surface-two-looks state D-03 was meant to remove. D-01 calls editor modals 'windows', but the code treats their interior as a settings page, and the run does not record which rule wins for the interior. This predates the branch. | [minor] QC-01 (R-2, Ka0s Default looks Ka0s) settings/IconBrowser.lua:124-126 ('colored, never given a face, so the font object's face stays under every skin (X10; ...)'); docs/smoke-tests.md SKIN-3 ('keep Blizzard's font under every skin'): Under Ka0s Default the icon browser is now the Ka0s window, but its count, status, hint and name lines still draw the client's font-object face (Friz Quadrata). The comment still cites X10 as the reason, and X10 was the stock-look-under-Ka0s-Default decision that R-2/D-06 superseded. Keeping Friz inside a Ka0s-chromed window goes against R-2's 'Ka0s Default looks like Ka0s Default everywhere'. No decision covers it: D-13 only covers library strings. The implementer flagged this as doubtful. | [nit] QC-RV test change tests/test_callout_popup.lua:289-296 (ownBackdrop reads LibStub("LibKa0s-Skin-1.0").__ours): The test reads a double-underscore library internal (`__ours`, SkinPaint.lua:128). It works and does not pass vacuously: without `__ours` the Shell holder would be flagged. But a test-only reach into internals is the pattern D-02/D-16 steer away from, and it breaks silently in meaning if the library renames the registry. | [nit] QC-01 test coverage tests/test_skin_tools.lua:429-436 (source scan over six settings files): The scan that requires every settings-page NS.SkinColor/NS.SkinEscape call to carry the panel flag lists six files by hand (Editors, Groups, Groups_When, Dials, Pads, Callouts). A new unflagged call in another settings page file (Panel.lua, Profiles.lua, Schema*.lua, OptionsSetup.lua) would not be caught. | [nit] QC-01 test comment accuracy tests/test_skin_hud.lua:327-340 ('a stored pick LibSharedMedia lacks (Friz Quadrata, the default) is the standard face'): In the client, LibSharedMedia registers "Friz Quadrata TT" (except on koKR/zhCN/zhTW), so the claim is true only of the headless mock. The assertion is fine, but the wording could mislead a later reader about in-game behaviour. SKIN-12 states the in-game result correctly. | [nit] QC-02 docs docs/settings-panel.md, the callout popup section ('its Bg is lifted over the click-catcher's dim ... so the popup is as bright as the settings window'): Since v1.81.0, the template's Bg art is faded under the skin's window fill under every Ka0s skin, Ka0s Default included. The 'as bright as the settings window' rationale now holds only under EllesmereUI, where Bg is not faded. The passage reads as if Bg were still what the player sees.
Fix round: Both findings are real, and both are fixed in the one commit 20ab5a7 on feat/2026-10-30-skin-library-followups in /mnt/d/Profile/Users/Tushar/Documents/GIT/QuickComms. Nothing was pushed, tagged, merged or version-bumped, and no other repo was touched. CRLF files were edited byte-safe through Python, and the eol test passes.

Finding 1 (popup body mixed): fixed. The popup's interior is a CreatePanel settings page inside the skinned window, so its AceGUI rows stand down under the default Ignore settings panel. The refused-field line and the token tooltip are that page's own lines, so they now follow the panel Scope too: the window follows D-01 and the page inside it follows D-03. `settings/CalloutPopup.lua` now calls `NS.SkinEscape(ERROR_TOKEN, true)` and `NS.SkinColor("gtip.value"/"gtip.title", true)`. The comments in CalloutPopup and ModalWindow say this, as do docs/settings-panel.md and SKIN-8. SKIN-8 gains the expectation, cross-referenced to EDIT-9 and EDIT-18. No shipped skin moves sem.badAlt or gtip.value today, so the bug could not show in practice yet. The tests in tests/test_skin_windows.lua therefore register a probe skin ("qcprobe") to tell the two reads apart: one changed case and one new case, both red before the fix.

Finding 2 (icon browser keeps Friz under Ka0s Default): fixed by taking the finding's first option instead of adding a DECISIONS entry. The finding wanted the lead to choose between routing these texts through the skin's face and recording a decision to keep Friz; I picked the first without a lead ruling. If the lead wants Friz kept, reverting `paintText` is a small change. The count, status, hint and name lines go back through `NS.Skin.Text(fs, token)`, the library's recorded paint. They take the token's color plus the skin's body face, so Ka0s Default draws Oxanium inside its window, and Ignore fonts gives the font-object face back (the library's `P.font`/`P.unfont`). This undoes 83b8dc0, whose only basis was X10, and X10 was retired by R-2/D-06. The comment now cites R-2, and the manual texts-repaint loop is removed. SKIN-3 no longer says these lines keep Blizzard's font: they move to its list of expected changes as Oxanium, with Ignore fonts restoring Blizzard's. SKIN-8 says the texts take the skin's body font. The test now checks that the lines are never given a face under Ignore fonts and take the face live once it is lifted, under Ka0s Default and under another skin. The mock cannot model restoring a template's font object, so the restore itself is not checked.

Also updated: README badge 1260/1260 and docs/test-cases.md regenerated. One HUD item is left alone: modules/Dial_Render.lua's hub labels also keep their font objects' faces. That is outside these findings and was not changed.
